While a game hides the cursor and holds the pointer, a mouse's own counts go to it as Windows' raw input does, with none of macOS's pointer acceleration; the trackpad keeps today's path.

This repository's patch to the Mac driver (version 1), after Recall's wine-mouselook.patch (github.com/AsherJN/recall
at 7aa7fb3, Josh Nelson; a change to Wine, LGPL-2.1-or-later): its design (GameController's GCMouse while the game
holds the pointer, the Mac pointer stopped, 1 ms batching, AppKit's moves dropped while the mouse reports, clicks and
scrolls that don't move Wine's cursor, GetCursorPos left to Wine's server), written for this tree. To be offered to
WineHQ. Needs 0013's clientWantsCursorHidden, 0015's overlay lift and restore (v4: Show Desktop too) and 0036's
GameController link.

The fault: a game that holds the pointer (mouse-look) got [NSEvent deltaX], the Mac's accelerated pointer motion in
points, coalesced to about one move per display frame (winit measured 8 ms between AppKit moves against 1 ms at the
device), and lost the moves stamped before each of its own SetCursorPos warps. Windows games expect raw counts: the
same hand movement turned the camera by different amounts at different speeds (mavrik S210, the user's Skyrim walk).

- Raw mode is on while all of these hold, re-evaluated when any of them changes: Wine is the active app; the key
  window is a visible WineWindow and none is being dragged; the game hides the cursor (0013's state); the confinement
  clip holds the pointer and 0015 hasn't lifted it for an overlay; a GameController mouse that is not a trackpad is
  connected; the Mac driver option RawMouseInput isn't "n" (default on, not shown to players: for lab comparisons and
  the per-game fix list). Only with the confinement clip, because 0015's watch runs then: Mission Control, App Exposé
  and Show Desktop lift the clip and so end raw mode, and the player has the pointer there. A game that holds the
  pointer by warping alone keeps AppKit's path, as does every Mac with only a trackpad (the player's choice: the
  trackpad keeps macOS's acceleration).
- On: CGAssociateMouseAndMouseCursorPosition(false) stops the Mac pointer; each counted mouse's mouseMovedHandler
  runs on one serial queue at user-interactive priority (cocoa_rawmouse.m) and adds the counts (GameController's Y
  axis points up); at most once a millisecond the whole counts go to the key window's queue as one MOUSE_MOVED_RELATIVE,
  the remainders kept. Wine's server puts relative moves into WM_INPUT unchanged and moves its cursor by them. Counts
  are never scaled for Retina mode: Windows' raw input is in counts at any DPI.
- AppKit's moves during raw mode: dropped while a counted mouse reported in the last 250 ms (the same motion); others
  (the trackpad) go as relative moves with macOS's acceleration, as when the pointer is pinned at the clip's edge. The
  hidden cursor is still set again (0014 v2).
- SetCursorPos in raw mode warps nothing and drops no queued move; the position is kept, and GetCursorPos answers
  Wine's own position. Clicks and wheel turns carry no position (no_move), so the stopped pointer never moves Wine's
  cursor.
- Off (any condition gone, resign active first of all): the handlers are cleared, the pointer associated again and
  put where the game last put its cursor, unless macOS shows an overlay; the next AppKit move is absolute. The stopped
  pointer is this process's window-server state and ends with it.

The mouse-or-trackpad test is one function, raw_mouse_device_counts() (Recall's: the device's vendorName names a
Trackpad).

Checked (mavrik S217 lab): applies after patches 0001 to 0038 with no fuzz; winemac builds with the engine's toolchain
with no new warning. Its effect needs a mouse, the screen and a game: mavrik's away block and walk.

